Body:
Quick source dive — the likely culprit is in `packages/opencode/src/index.ts`:

https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/9211ef7e95b7cd55f08b27b066d635cc42cbb362/packages/opencode/src/index.ts#L202-L208

```ts
} finally {
  // Some subprocesses don't react properly to SIGTERM and similar signals.
  // ...
  // Explicitly exit to avoid any hanging subprocesses.
  process.exit()
}
```

The `try { await cli.parse() }` block runs yargs, which for the `completion` subcommand writes the generated shell script directly to `process.stdout`. Control then falls into `finally`, which calls `process.exit()` unconditionally. Node's `process.exit()` does not wait for writable streams to drain — when stdout is a pipe (as with `eval "$(opencode completion)"`), the kernel-side pipe buffer isn't fully consumed before the process is torn down, so the tail of the output is dropped. That's how the script ends mid-string with the unmatched `"`.

This is consistent with the other reports of this race (#29866 JSON stream, #26114/#28606 `--help` newline, #29330 `export | jq`, #26399 `debug skill`), and looks like exactly what closed PR #29340 ("fix(opencode): flush stdio before cli exit") was attempting to address.

### Suggested fix

Drain stdio before exiting, e.g.:

```ts
} finally {
  await Promise.all(
    [process.stdout, process.stderr].map(
      (s) => new Promise<void>((resolve) =>
        s.write("") ? resolve() : s.once("drain", () => resolve()),
      ),
    ),
  )
  process.exit()
}
```

Setting `process.exitCode` and letting Node exit naturally would also work, but conflicts with the existing rationale for the explicit `process.exit()` (hanging subprocesses), so an explicit drain-then-exit is the safer change.

